17592186045417 true] [17592186151826 1074 "Let $x$ be the loaf's weight (in ounces), and $y$ be the time (in minutes) the loaf needs to bake.\n\nFirst, we need to construct the equation that represents the proportional relationship. Every proportional relationship takes the form $y=kx$, where $k$ is the constant of proportionality.\n\nWe are told that if the loaf weighs $x=12$ ounces, then Amy needs to bake it for $y=40$ minutes. We can find the constant of proportionality by computing the ratio of the $y$-value to the $x$-value, as follows:\n\n$$\nk = \\dfrac{y}{x} = \\dfrac{40}{12} = \\dfrac{40 \\div 4}{12 \\div 4} = \\dfrac{10}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation is\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{10}{3} x\n$$\n\nNow, we want to find the value of $x$ so that the loaf will bake for exactly $y=60$ minutes. So, we substitute $y=60$ into the equation above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n60 &= \\frac{10}{3}x \\\\\n\\frac{10}{3}x &= 60\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve for $x$, we multiply both sides of the equation by the reciprocal of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{10}{3}\n$$\n\nwhich is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{10}\n$$\n\nWe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{3}{10} \\cdot \\frac{10}{3}x &= \\frac{3}{10} \\cdot 60 \\\\\n1x &= \\frac{3 \\cdot 60}{10} \\\\\nx &= \\frac{180}{10} \\\\\nx &= 18\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the loaf of bread should weigh $18$ ounces." 13194139535339 17592186045417 false] [17592186151826 1074 "Let $x$ be the loaf's weight (in ounces), and $y$ be the time (in minutes) the loaf needs to bake.\n\nFirst, we need to construct the equation that represents the proportional relationship. Every proportional relationship takes the form $y=kx,$ where $k$ is the constant of proportionality.\n\nWe are told that if the loaf weighs $x=12$ ounces, then Amy needs to bake it for $y=40$ minutes. We can find the constant of proportionality by computing the ratio of the $y$-value to the $x$-value, as follows:\n\n$$\nk = \\dfrac{y}{x} = \\dfrac{40}{12} = \\dfrac{40 \\div 4}{12 \\div 4} = \\dfrac{10}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation is\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{10}{3} x.\n$$\n\nNow, we want to find the value of $x$ so that the loaf will bake for exactly $y=60$ minutes. So, we substitute $y=60$ into the equation above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}60 & =\\frac{10}{3}x \\\\ \\frac{10}{3}x & =60\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve for $x,$ we multiply both sides of the equation by the reciprocal of $\\dfrac{10}{3},$ which is $\\dfrac{3}{10}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{10}\\times \\frac{10}{3}x & =\\frac{3}{10}\\times 60 \\\\ 1x & =\\frac{3\\times 60}{10} \\\\ x & =\\frac{180}{10} \\\\ x & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the loaf of bread should weigh $18$ ounces." 13194139535339 17592186045417 true] [17592186151920 1074 "Remember that the graph of a proportional relationship is always a straight line that passes through the origin $(0,0)$.\n\nOf the given graphs, only graph II fits this description.\n\n- Graphs I and IV are not straight lines.\n- Graph III does not pass through the origin $(0,0)$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." 13194139535339 17592186045417 false] [17592186151920 1074 "Remember that the graph of a proportional relationship is always a straight line that passes through the origin $(0,0).$\n\nOf the given graphs, only graph II fits this description.\n\n- Graphs I and IV are not straight lines.\n\n- Graph III does not pass through the origin $(0,0).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." 13194139535339 17592186045417 true] [17592186151921 1074 "To find the constant of proportionality, we first need to choose a point $(x,y)$ on the graph. Here, let's choose the point $(4,3)$.\n\n![](images/d5/d573d9aad231622b2e5dc42a8672d973e5fda518d75894f3bafb0b1a97532d24.png)\n\nNow, we can find the constant of proportionality by computing the ratio of the $y$-coordinate to the $x$-coordinate, as follows:\n\n$$\nk = \\dfrac{y}{x} = \\dfrac{3}{4}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the constant of proportionality is\n\n$$\nk=\\dfrac{3}{4}\n$$" 131941395