-- | ---: |\n| $0$ |  |\n| $1$ |  |\n| $2$ |  |\n| $3$ |  |\n\nFilling in the $y$-values using the equation $y = 2x$, we get the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $0$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $2$ |\n| $2$ | $4$ |\n| $3$ | $6$ |\n\nSo, we have the following $(x,y)$ coordinates:\n\n$$\n(0,0), \\quad (1,2), \\quad (2,4), \\quad (3,6)\n$$\n\nIf we plot these points in the coordinate plane, we get the following:\n\n![](images/a8/a8cd951c66c67b8f28b791011c7553675b3112029d9ebe4b19769127b5e75bd5.png)\n\nThis leads us to the following important fact:\n\n> *The graph of any proportional relationship is a straight line that passes through the origin $(0,0)$.*" 13194139535339 17592186045417 false] [17592186153338 1064 "How does a proportional relationship look when we plot it in the $(x,y)$ plane?\n\nTo answer this, let's consider the following proportional relationship:\n\n$$\ny = 2x\n$$\n\nTo plot a graph, we first need a table of values. \n\n| $x$ | $y$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $0$ |  |\n| $1$ |  |\n| $2$ |  |\n| $3$ |  |\n\nFilling in the $y$-values using the equation $y = 2x,$ we get the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $2$ |\n| $2$ | $4$ |\n| $3$ | $6$ |\n\nSo, we have the following $(x,y)$ coordinates:\n\n$$\n(0,0), \\quad (1,2),  \\quad (2,4),  \\quad (3,6)\n$$\n\nIf we plot these points in the coordinate plane, we get the following:\n\n![](images/a8/a8cd951c66c67b8f28b791011c7553675b3112029d9ebe4b19769127b5e75bd5.png)\n\nThis leads us to the following important fact:\n\n*The graph of any proportional relationship is a straight line that passes through the origin $(0,0)$.*" 13194139535339 17592186045417 true] [17592186153339 1064 "When there is a proportional relationship between $x$ and $y$, we can express this using the **proportionality symbol** $\\propto$, as follows:\n$y \\propto x$\n\nNote that the following statements are all equivalent:\n\n- $y = kx$\n- $y$ **varies directly** with $x$\n- $y$ is **directly proportional** to $x$\n- $y \\propto x$" 13194139535339 17592186045417 false] [17592186153339 1064 "When there is a proportional relationship between $x$ and $y,$ we can express this using the **proportionality symbol** $\\propto,$ as follows:\n\n$$\ny \\propto x\n$$\n\nNote that the following statements are all equivalent:\n\n- $y = kx$\n\n- $y$ **varies directly** with $x$\n\n- $y$ is **directly proportional** to $x$\n\n- $y \\propto x$" 13194139535339 17592186045417 true] [17592186153437 1064 "Recall that two variables, $x$ and $y$, are said to have a proportional relationship if their ratio is constant. The constant of proportionality $k$ is given by\n\n$$\nk = \\dfrac{y}{x}\n$$\n\nWe can rearrange the equation above as follows:\n\n$$\ny = kx\n$$\n\nThe following statements are all equivalent:\n\n- $y = kx$\n- $y$ **varies directly** with $x$\n- $y$ is **directly proportional** to $x$\n- $y \\propto x$\n\nProportional relationships frequently occur in real-world problems. Let's see an example." 13194139535339 17592186045417 false] [17592186153437 1064 "Recall that two variables, $x$ and $y,$ are said to have a proportional relationship if their ratio is constant. The constant of proportionality $k$ is given by\n\n$$\nk = \\dfrac{y}{x}.\n$$\n\nWe can rearrange the equation above as follows:\n\n$$\ny = kx\n$$\n\nThe following statements are all equivalent:\n\n- $y = kx$\n\n- $y$ **varies directly** with $x$\n\n- $y$ is **directly proportional** to $x$\n\n- $y \\propto x$\n\nProportional relationships frequently occur in real-world problems. Let's see an example." 13194139535339 17592186045417 true] [17592186159524 1078 #edb/ref 17592186159527 13194139535339 17592186045417 true] [17592186162109 1038 #edb/ref 17592186498514 13194139535339 17592186045417 true] [17592186162110 1038 #edb/ref 17592186498591 13194139535339 17592186045417 true] [17592186162111 1038 #edb/ref 17592186498499 13194139535339 17592186045417 true] [17592186162111 1038 #edb/ref 17592186498578 13194139535339 17592186045417 true] [17592186162387 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535339 17592186045417 true] [17592186162387 1071 "The weights of a particular breed of geese are normally distributed with a mean of $7\\,\\text{kg}$ and a standard deviation of $1\\,\\text{kg}.$ Suppose $4$ geese are randomly selected. What is the probability that the mean weight for this sample will be more than $6.8\\,\\text{kg},$ but less than $7\\,\\text{kg}?$\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.00\\,$</th>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.01\\,$</th>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.02\\,$</th>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.4\\,$</th>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3446\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3409\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3372\\,$ </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.3\\,$</th>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3821\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3783\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3745\\,$ </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.2\\,$</th>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4207\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4168\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4129\\,$ </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p></p></th>\n<th width=\"5%\"></th>\n<th><p></p>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.00\\,$</th>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.01\\,$</th>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.02\\,$</th>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.1\\,$</th>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4602\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4562\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4522\\,$ </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.0\\,$</th>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.5000\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4960\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4920\\,$ </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.1\\,$</th>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.5398\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.5359\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.5319\\,$ </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p></p></th>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" 13194139535339 17592186045417 true] [17592186162387 1071 "The weights of a particular breed of geese are normally distributed with a mean of $7kg$ and a standard deviation of $1kg$. Suppose $4$ geese are randomly selected. What is the probability that the mean weight for this sample will be more than $6.8kg$, but less than $7kg$?\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $z$$0.00$$0.01$$0.02$$-0.4$ $0.3446$ $0.3409$ $0.3372$ $-0.3$ $0.3821$ $0.3783$ $0.3745$ $-0.2$ $0.4207$ $0.4168$ $0.4129$ |  | $z$$0.00$$0.01$$0.02$$-0.1$ $0.4602$ $0.4562$ $0.4522$ $0.0$ $0.5000$ $0.4960$ $0.4920$ $0.1$ $0.5398$ $0.5359$ $0.5319$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139535339 17592186045417 false] [17592186162387 1074 "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a normal population, where \n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(\\mu ,{\\sigma }^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le n,\n$$\n\n then the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ has the distribution\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nWe're told that the population mean $\\mu =7,$ the population standard deviation $\\sigma =1,$ and that the sample size $n=4.$ Therefore, the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(7,\\frac{{1}^{2}}{4}) \\\\  & ∼N(7,0.25).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're required to find $P(6.8<\\overset{―}{X}<7).$\n\nFirst, we transform $X$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(6.8<\\overset{―}{X}<7) & =P(\\frac{6.8-7}{\\sqrt{0.25}}<Z<\\frac{7-7}{\\sqrt{0.25}}) \\\\  & =P(-0.4<Z<0) \\\\  & =Φ(0)-Φ(-0.4) \\\\  & =0.5-Φ(-0.4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(-0.4)=0.3446.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(6.8<\\overset{―}{X}<7) & =0.5-Φ(-0.4) \\\\  & =0.5-0.3446 \\\\  & =0.1554.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535339 17592186045417 true] [17592186171069 1075 #edb/ref 17592186171071 13194139535339 17592186045417 false] [17592186171069 1075 #edb/ref 17592186171072 13194139535339 17592186045417 false] [17592186171069 1075 #edb/ref 17592186171073 13194139535339 17592186045417 false] [17592186171069 1075 #edb/ref 17592186171074 13194139535339 17592186045417 false] [17592186171069 1075 #edb/ref 17592186497838 13194139535339 17592186045417 true] [17592186171069 1075 #edb/ref 17592186497851 13194139535339 17592186045417 true] [17592186171069 1075 #edb/ref 17592186498062 13194139535339 17592186045417 true] [17592186171069 1075 #edb/ref 17592186498231 13194139535339 17592186045417 true] [17592186171069 1078 #edb/ref 17592186497838 13194139535339 17592186045417 true] [17592186171714 1071 "Simplify the expression $\\left| - \\dfrac{x^2}{10} \\right|$." 13194139535339 17592186045417 false] [17592186171714 1071 "Simplify the expression $\\left| - \\dfrac{x^2}{10} \\right|.$" 13194139535339 17592186045417 true] [17592186171714 1074 "Firstly, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}{-a} & = & a\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nwe can write our expression as\n\n$$\n\\left| \\dfrac{x^2}{10} \\right|\n$$\n\nThen, we distribute the absolute value over the multiplication and division:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid \\frac{x^{2}}{10} \\mid &=  \\\\