\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =4\\cdot 4+9\\cdot 1+16\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =73.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y=2{X}_{1}-3{X}_{2}+4{X}_{3} & ∼N(9,73).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535339 17592186045417 true] [17592186176289 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535339 17592186045417 true] [17592186176289 1071 "A course grade is calculated by adding the results of a written test, oral test, and colloquium, contributing $50%$, $30%$, and $20%$, respectively to the final grade.\n\nThe written test results are normally distributed with a mean of $64$ and a standard deviation of $8$. The oral test results are normally distributed with a mean of $50$ and a standard deviation of $10$, while the results of the colloquium are normally distributed with a mean of $60$ and a standard deviation of $10$. Find the probability that a randomly selected student achieves a grade greater than $70$. You may assume that the scores on each test are independent.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $z$ | $0.00$ | $0.01$ | $0.02$ | $0.03$ | $0.04$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1.9$ | $0.9713$ | $0.9719$ | $0.9726$ | $0.9732$ | $0.9738$ |\n| $2.0$ | $0.9772$ | $0.9778$ | $0.9783$ | $0.9788$ | $0.9793$ |\n| $2.1$ | $0.9821$ | $0.9826$ | $0.9830$ | $0.9834$ | $0.9838$ |\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139535339 17592186045417 false] [17592186176289 1071 "A course grade is calculated by adding the results of a written test, oral test, and colloquium, contributing $50%$, $30%,$ and $20%,$ respectively to the final grade. \n\nThe written test results are normally distributed with a mean of $64$ and a standard deviation of $8.$ The oral test results are normally distributed with a mean of $50$ and a standard deviation of $10$, while the results of the colloquium are normally distributed with a mean of $60$ and a standard deviation of $10.$ Find the probability that a randomly selected student achieves a grade greater than $70.$ You may assume that the scores on each test are independent.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,1.9\\,$ | $\\,0.9713\\,$ | $\\,0.9719\\,$ | $\\,0.9726\\,$ | $\\,0.9732\\,$ | $\\,0.9738\\,$ |\n| $\\,2.0\\,$ | $\\,0.9772\\,$ | $\\,0.9778\\,$ | $\\,0.9783\\,$ | $\\,0.9788\\,$ | $\\,0.9793\\,$ |\n| $\\,2.1\\,$ | $\\,0.9821\\,$ | $\\,0.9826\\,$ | $\\,0.9830\\,$ | $\\,0.9834\\,$ | $\\,0.9838\\,$ |" 13194139535339 17592186045417 true] [17592186176289 1074 "Let ${X}_{1}$, ${X}_{2},$ and ${X}_{3}$ denote the written test, oral test, and colloquium results, respectively. Then, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{1}∼N(64,{8}^{2}),\\,{X}_{2}∼N(50,{10}^{2}),\\,{X}_{3}∼N(60,{10}^{2}).\n$$\n\nLet the random variable $Y$ denote the final grade of a randomly selected student. Since the written, oral, and colloquium contribute $50%$, $30%,$ and $20%$, respectively, to the final grade, we have\n\n$$\nY=0.5{X}_{1}+0.3{X}_{2}+0.2{X}_{3}.\n$$\n\nThe random variable $Y$ is normally distributed, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & =0.5\\cdot 64+0.3\\cdot 50+0.2\\cdot 60 \\\\  & =59, \\\\ \\text{Var}[Y] & =(0.5)^{2}\\cdot {8}^{2}+(0.3)^{2}\\cdot {10}^{2}+(0.2)^{2}\\cdot {10}^{2} \\\\  & =0.25\\cdot 64+0.09\\cdot 10+0.04\\cdot 100 \\\\  & =29.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y & ∼N(59,29).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order to find $P(Y>70),$ we convert $Y$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y>70) & =P(Z>\\frac{70-59}{\\sqrt{29}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>2.04) \\\\  & =1-P(Z<2.04) \\\\  & =1-Φ(2.04)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\nΦ(2.04)≈0.9793.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y>70) & ≈1-Φ(2.04) \\\\  & ≈1-0.9793 \\\\  & =0.0207.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535339 17592186045417 true] [17592186176415 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535339 17592186045417 true] [17592186176415 1071 "Solve for $x$ where $\\frac{1}{x - 2} = \\frac{2}{x + 4}$.$x =$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535339 17592186045417 false] [17592186176415 1071 "Solve for $x$ where $\\frac{1}{x-2}=\\frac{2}{x+4}.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535339 17592186045417 true] [17592186176415 1074 "First, note that $x=-4$ and $x=2$ cannot be solutions since division by $0$ is undefined.\n\nTo solve the equation, we first apply cross-multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x-2} & =\\frac{2}{x+4} \\\\ 1\\cdot (x+4) & =(x-2)\\cdot 2 \\\\ x+4 & =2x-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles, as usual:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+4 & =2x-4 \\\\ x+4-x & =2x-4-x \\\\ 4 & =x-4 \\\\ 4+4 & =x-4+4 \\\\ 8 & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $x=8$ is different from $x=-4$ and $x=2,$ we can accept it as a solution. Therefore, the solution is $x=8.$" 13194139535339 17592186045417 true] [17592186177005 1064 "When we form a random variable $Y$ from a linear combination of mutually independent, normally distributed random variables, the distribution of $Y$ is also normal.\n\nMore precisely, we have the following theorem:\n\n*If $X_1, X_2,\\ldots, X_n$ are mutually independent normal random variables such that*\n\n$$\nX_1 \\sim N(\\mu_1,\\sigma_1^2), \\qquad X_2\\sim (\\mu_2,\\sigma_2^2), \\qquad \\ldots, \\quad X_n\\sim(\\mu_n,\\sigma_n^2),\n$$\n\n \n\n*and $a_1,a_2,\\ldots,a_n$ are real constants, then the linear combination* \n\n$$\nY=a_1X_1+a_2X_2+\\ldots+a_nX_n\n$$\n\n \n\n*is normally distributed, where*\n\n$$\nY\\sim N(a_1\\mu_1+a_2\\mu_2+\\ldots+a_n\\mu_n, \\: a_1^2\\sigma_1^2+a_2^2\\sigma_2^2+\\ldots+a_n^2\\sigma_n^2).\n$$\n\nFor example, if the random variables\n\n$$\nX_1\\sim N({\\color{blue}2},{\\color{red}3}), \\qquad X_2\\sim  N({\\color{blue}1},{\\color{red}4}), \\qquad X_3\\sim N({\\color{blue}1},{\\color{red}1})\n$$\n\nare mutually independent, then \n\n$$\nY= \\bbox[2pt,AntiqueWhite]{3}X_1+\\bbox[2pt,AntiqueWhite]{2}X_2+\\bbox[2pt,AntiqueWhite]{4}X_3\n$$\n\nis normally distributed. \n\nLet's figure out the distribution of $Y.$ We start by computing its mean and variance:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & ={a}_{1}{\\mu }_{1}+{a}_{2}{\\mu }_{2}+{a}_{3}{\\mu }_{3} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2+2\\cdot 1+4\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =6+2+4 \\\\  & =12, \\\\ \\text{Var}[Y] & ={a}_{1}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{a}_{2}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}+{a}_{3}^{2}{\\sigma }_{3}^{2} \\\\  & ={3}^{2}\\cdot 3+{2}^{2}\\cdot 4+{4}^{2}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =9\\cdot 3+4\\cdot 4+16\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =27+16+16 \\\\  & =59.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y=3{X}_{1}+2{X}_{2}+4{X}_{3} & ∼N(12,59).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** It is vital that $X_1, X_2,\\ldots X_n$ are *mutually independent*. If they are *not*, their linear combination may *not* be normally distributed!" 13194139535339 17592186045417 true] [17592186177005 1064 "When we form a random variable $Y$ from a linear combination of mutually independent, normally distributed random variables, the distribution of $Y$ is also normal.\n\nMore precisely, we have the following theorem:\n\n> *If $X_1, X_2,\\ldots, X_n$ are mutually independent normal random variables such that*\n\n$$\nX_1 \\sim N(\\mu_1,\\sigma_1^2), \\qquad X_2\\sim (\\mu_2,\\sigma_2^2), \\qquad \\ldots, \\quad X_n\\sim(\\mu_n,\\sigma_n^2)\n$$\n\n> *and $a_1,a_2,\\ldots,a_n$ are real constants, then the linear combination*\n\n$$\nY=a_1X_1+a_2X_2+\\ldots+a_nX_n\n$$\n\n> *is normally distributed, where*\n> $Y\\sim N(a_1\\mu_1+a_2\\mu_2+\\ldots+a_n\\mu_n, \\: a_1^2\\sigma_1^2+a_2^2\\sigma_2^2+\\ldots+a_n^2\\sigma_n^2)$.\n\nFor example, if the random variables\n\n$$\nX_1\\sim N({\\color{blue}2},{\\color{red}3}), \\qquad X_2\\sim N({\\color{blue}1},{\\color{red}4}), \\qquad X_3\\sim N({\\color{blue}1},{\\color{red}1})\n$$\n\nare mutually independent, then\n\n$$\nY= \\bbox[2pt,AntiqueWhite]{3}X_1+\\bbox[2pt,AntiqueWhite]{2}X_2+\\bbox[2pt,AntiqueWhite]{4}X_3\n$$\n\nis normally distributed.\n\nLet's figure out the distribution of $Y$. We start by computing its mean and variance:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[Y] &= a_{1}μ_{1} + a_{2}μ_{2} + a_{3}μ_{3} \\\\\n&= 3 \\cdot 2 + 2 \\cdot 1 + 4 \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= 6 + 2 + 4 \\\\\n&= 12, \\\\\nVar[Y] &= a_{1}^{2}σ_{1}^{2} + a_{2}^{2}σ_{2}^{2} + a_{3}^{2}σ_{3}^{2} \\\\\n&= 3^{2} \\cdot 3 + 2^{2} \\cdot 4 + 4^{2} \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= 9 \\cdot 3 + 4 \\cdot 4 + 16 \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= 27 + 16 + 16 \\\\\n&= 59\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nY = 3X_{1} + 2X_{2} + 4X_{3} \\mid ∼N(12, 59)\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** It is vital that $X_1, X_2,\\ldots X_n$ are *mutually independent*. If they are *not*, their linear combination may *not* be normally distributed!" 13194139535339 17592186045417 false] [17592186177055 1064 "When we form a random variable $Y$ from a sum of **mutually independent**, normally distributed random variables, the distribution of $Y$ is also normal.\n\nMore precisely, we have the following theorem:\n\n*If $X_1, X_2,\\ldots, X_n$ are mutually independent normal random variables such that*\n\n$$\nX_1 \\sim N(\\mu_1,\\sigma_1^2), \\qquad X_2\\sim (\\mu_2,\\sigma_2^2), \\qquad \\ldots, \\quad X_n\\sim(\\mu_n,\\sigma_n^2),\n$$\n\n \n\n*then the random variable* \n\n$$\nY=X_1+X_2+\\ldots+X_n\n$$\n\n \n\n*is normally distributed, where*\n\n$$\nY\\sim N(\\mu_1+\\mu_2+\\ldots+\\mu_n, \\: \\sigma_1^2+\\sigma_2^2+\\ldots+\\sigma_n^2).\n$$\n\nFor example, if the random variables\n\n$$\nX_1\\sim N({\\color{blue}2},{\\color{red}3}), \\qquad X_2\\sim  N({\\color{blue}1},{\\color{red}4}), \\qquad X_3\\sim N({\\color{blue}1},{\\color{red}1})\n$$\n\nare mutually independent, then \n\n$$\nY=X_1+X_2+X_3\n$$\n\nis normally distributed. \n\nLet's figure out the distribution of $Y.$ We start by computing