 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -11 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}-11 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}-11 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-1)\\cdot (-11)-4\\cdot 3}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 \\\\ -4 & -11\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 11\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can compute the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-11 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 11\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}20 & -6 \\\\ -7 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 11\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -6 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535339 17592186045417 true] [17592186395256 1074 "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](images/1b/1b81504d6fdcabee0e1f586fef3dd2af5a57a8411ba4d22429e00e09b171a4d1.png)\n\n \n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$\n\nFirst, let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of vectors from $B$ relative to the basis $C.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ relative to the